# Race Buddy - Race Build Tracker & Running Diary

## Project Overview

Race Buddy is a personal tool for a hobbyist runner: track the races you're
targeting, plan and evolve the training build for each one, log how every
workout actually went (completed, actual miles, execution rating, notes),
manage race-weekend logistics (hotel, travel, expo, schedule, gear), and keep
a running diary throughout the build.

**Tech Stack:**
- Framework: Next.js (App Router) with React 19
- Styling: Tailwind CSS, Shadcn UI, Radix UI
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL, RLS)

---

## General Development Standards

### Code Style and Structure
- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples.
- Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes.
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication.
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError).
- Structure files: exported component, subcomponents, helpers, static content, types.

### Naming Conventions
- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard).
- Favor named exports for components.

### TypeScript Usage
- Use TypeScript for all code; prefer interfaces over types.
- Avoid enums; use maps instead (see `src/lib/constants.ts`).
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces.

### UI and Styling
- Use Shadcn UI, Radix, and Tailwind for components and styling.
- UI primitives live in `src/components/ui/`; feature components in
  `src/components/`.
- Mobile-first responsive design with Tailwind breakpoints.
- Semantic zinc palette with per-domain accents (workout type badge colors
  are defined once in `src/lib/constants.ts`).

### Server vs Client Components
- Default to React Server Components; pages fetch data server-side and pass
  it to client components as props.
- Use `"use client"` only for interactivity (forms, toggles, logging UI).
- After a mutation, client components call `router.refresh()` so server
  components re-fetch.

---

## Architecture

### Data Model (see supabase-migrations.sql)
- `races` — target events: name, race_date, distance, location, goal,
  race_url, notes, result_time, result_notes.
- `workouts` — the build: race_id, workout_date, type
  (easy|long|tempo|intervals|race_pace|cross|rest), title, planned_miles,
  description, status (planned|completed|skipped), actual_miles,
  execution_rating (1–5), log_notes, completed_at.
- `logistics_items` — race weekend: category
  (hotel|travel|expo|schedule|gear|other), title, details, item_date, url, done.
- `diary_entries` — journal: optional race_id, entry_date, title, body, mood.

### Supabase Patterns
- Use `createServerClient()` from `@/lib/supabase` in Server Components and
  API routes; it is created lazily so the app renders a setup notice when
  env vars are missing (`isSupabaseConfigured()`).
- Always specify exact columns in `.select()` — never `select('*')`.
- Destructure `{ data, error }`, log errors, and fall back to empty arrays.
- RLS allows public reads; writes go through API routes using the service
  role key.

### Mutations
- All writes go through the generic routes `POST /api/[resource]` and
  `PATCH|DELETE /api/[resource]/[id]`.
- Tables and writable columns are allowlisted in `src/lib/resources.ts`;
  extend that file when adding a new resource or column.
- Client-side, use `createRecord` / `updateRecord` / `deleteRecord` from
  `@/lib/api`.

### Dates
- Dates are `YYYY-MM-DD` strings; always parse with `parseDate()` from
  `@/lib/dates` (local-time safe), never `new Date(string)`.
- Weeks start on Monday; week grouping and build-week math live in
  `@/lib/dates`.

---

## Notes
- Keep the codebase simple and maintainable — this is a single-user tool.
- The training plan is meant to evolve: editing/rescheduling workouts is a
  first-class flow, not an edge case.
- Build incrementally — ship features quickly, then iterate.
